# Nodes across tabs

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kino, "~> 0.19"}])
```

## Connecting to another tab

This notebook connects to the same notebook in another browser tab with Erlang's distribution, then creates a slider that is synchronized between the nodes.

To try it, open this demo in another tab, open this notebook there too, and evaluate this cell in both tabs.

```elixir
{node(), Node.get_cookie()}
```

Then, in one tab, paste the other tab's node and cookie in place of the `nil`s, and evaluate the cell. Connecting from one tab is enough: `Node.list()` shows the connected nodes, here and in the other tab.

```elixir
{other_node, other_cookie} = {nil, nil}

if other_node do
  Node.set_cookie(other_node, other_cookie)
  Node.connect(other_node)
end

Node.list()
```

## A synchronized slider

Evaluate this cell in both tabs, then move one tab's slider: the other's follows. In each tab, a process named `:slider` shows the slider and its value in frames, and gets the slider's changes. When the slider moves, the process sends the value to the `:slider` processes of the connected nodes.

```elixir
defmodule Slider do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(frame), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, frame, name: :slider)

  @impl true
  def init(frame), do: {:ok, frame, {:continue, :show}}

  # After init, as Kino can't start frames while it's starting this process
  @impl true
  def handle_continue(:show, frame) do
    {slider_frame, value_frame} = {Kino.Frame.new(), Kino.Frame.new()}
    Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Layout.grid([slider_frame, value_frame], gap: 0))
    state = %{slider_frame: slider_frame, value_frame: value_frame, slider: nil}
    {:noreply, show_slider(state, 50)}
  end

  # Moved in this tab
  @impl true
  def handle_info({:moved, %{value: value}}, state) do
    show_value(state, value)
    for other <- Node.list(), do: send({:slider, other}, {:move, value})
    {:noreply, state}
  end

  # Moved in another tab
  def handle_info({:move, value}, state), do: {:noreply, show_slider(state, value)}

  defp show_slider(state, value) do
    if state.slider, do: Kino.Control.unsubscribe(state.slider)
    slider = Kino.Input.range("", default: value)
    Kino.Control.subscribe(slider, :moved)
    Kino.Frame.render(state.slider_frame, slider)
    show_value(state, value)
    %{state | slider: slider}
  end

  defp show_value(state, value) do
    Kino.Frame.render(state.value_frame, Kino.Text.new("Value: #{round(value)}"))
  end
end

frame = Kino.Frame.new()
Kino.start_child({Slider, frame})
frame
```
